## The question

The [Baltimore case](baltimore_case.livemd) predicted house prices with kriging, using three house
features as drift terms. Machine learning offers other tools for the same task: regularised linear
models, nearest neighbours, neural networks. Which works better here, and how should they be
**validated** when the data are spatial?

Two things make spatial data special for machine learning:

1. **Validation leaks through space.** With random folds, every test house has near-identical
   neighbours in the training set, so a model that memorises *places* looks better than it is.
   Validating by **spatial blocks** (whole areas left out) measures how a model does where it has
   not seen the neighbourhood.
2. **Residuals may stay spatially correlated.** A model of the house features leaves out what the
   location adds; the variogram of its residuals says whether there is something left for kriging
   (**regression kriging**: model + kriged residuals).

We use the 211 sales of 1978 (Dubin 1992, distributed with PySAL), Scholar (ridge regression,
nearest neighbours, k-means) and Axon (a small neural network).

## 1. Features and target

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame, as: DF
alias Explorer.Series, as: S
alias GeoFrame.GeoDataFrame, as: GDF
alias GeoFrame.GeoSeries, as: GS
alias Geostatex.{Kriging, Variogram}

sales = [__DIR__, "data", "baltimore", "baltim.shp"] |> Path.join() |> GeoFrame.read_file!()
data = GDF.data(sales)
column = fn name -> data[name] |> S.cast({:f, 64}) |> S.to_list() end

# The coordinates match the Maryland State Plane (NAD27, EPSG:26785) in thousands of US feet; maps
# draw them that way, the analysis keeps the map units (1 unit = 1000 ft, about 305 m).
on_map = fn frame ->
  {xs, ys} = Geostatex.GeoFrame.points(frame)
  geometry = GS.from_xy(Enum.map(xs, &(&1 * 1000)), Enum.map(ys, &(&1 * 1000)), crs: "EPSG:26785")
  GDF.new(GDF.data(frame), geometry)
end

features = ~w(SQFT LOTSZ AGE NROOM NBATH NSTOR PATIO FIREPL AC GAR BMENT DWELL CITCOU)
y = column.("PRICE") |> Enum.map(&:math.log/1)
xy = Enum.zip(column.("X"), column.("Y"))
x = features |> Enum.map(column) |> Enum.zip_with(& &1)
x_and_coords = Enum.zip_with(x, xy, fn row, {a, b} -> row ++ [a, b] end)
coords = Enum.map(xy, &Tuple.to_list/1)
n = length(y)

%{sales: n, features: features}
```

The target is the logarithm of the price. The 13 features are the living area, lot size, age,
rooms, bathrooms, stories, patio, fireplace, air conditioning, garage, basement, detached dwelling
and county (against city).

## 2. Two ways to validate

Five folds both times. **Random** folds shuffle the sales; **spatial** folds are five areas found
by k-means on the coordinates, each left out whole.

```elixir
{perm, _} = Nx.Random.shuffle(Nx.Random.key(7), Nx.iota({n}))
random_fold = perm |> Nx.to_list() |> Enum.with_index() |> Enum.sort() |> Enum.map(fn {_, i} -> rem(i, 5) end)

kmeans = Scholar.Cluster.KMeans.fit(Nx.tensor(coords), num_clusters: 5, key: Nx.Random.key(3))
spatial_fold = kmeans |> Scholar.Cluster.KMeans.predict(Nx.tensor(coords)) |> Nx.to_list()

sales = GDF.put(sales, "block", Enum.map(spatial_fold, &"block #{&1}"))
GeoFrame.Kino.deck(on_map.(sales), color: {"block", :categorical}, radius: 6, tooltip: ["block", "PRICE"])
```

Five blocks over an area of about 130 by 75 map units are each a few tens of units across, of the
order of the range of the price variogram (about 40): most test houses of a block have no close
neighbours in training.

## 3. Models

Every model gets the training rows of a fold and predicts the test rows; features are standardised
with the training rows' mean and deviation.

* **mean**: the training mean, no information at all;
* **ridge**: ridge regression (L2-regularised linear model) on the features, and on the features
  plus the coordinates;
* **KNN**: the mean of the 6 nearest training sales, location only;
* **MLP**: a neural network (two hidden layers of 32 and 16 ReLU units, dropout, AdamW with weight
  decay, 100 epochs, all seeded so that every run gives the same result), on the features and on
  the features plus the coordinates.

```elixir
standardise = fn train, test ->
  t = Nx.tensor(train)
  mu = Nx.mean(t, axes: [0])
  sd = t |> Nx.standard_deviation(axes: [0]) |> Nx.max(1.0e-9)
  scale = &Nx.divide(Nx.subtract(&1, mu), sd)
  {scale.(t), scale.(Nx.tensor(test))}
end

# Each model returns {test predictions, training predictions (or nil)}.
mean_model = fn _xtr, ytr, xte ->
  {List.duplicate(Enum.sum(ytr) / length(ytr), elem(Nx.shape(xte), 0)), nil}
end

ridge = fn xtr, ytr, xte ->
  m = Scholar.Linear.RidgeRegression.fit(xtr, Nx.tensor(ytr), alpha: 1.0)
  predict = &(m |> Scholar.Linear.RidgeRegression.predict(&1) |> Nx.to_flat_list())
  {predict.(xte), predict.(xtr)}
end

knn = fn xtr, ytr, xte ->
  m = Scholar.Neighbors.KNNRegressor.fit(xtr, Nx.reshape(Nx.tensor(ytr), {:auto, 1}), num_neighbors: 6)
  {m |> Scholar.Neighbors.KNNRegressor.predict(xte) |> Nx.to_flat_list(), nil}
end

mlp = fn xtr, ytr, xte ->
  model =
    Axon.input("x", shape: {nil, elem(Nx.shape(xtr), 1)})
    |> Axon.dense(32, activation: :relu)
    |> Axon.dropout(rate: 0.1, seed: 1)
    |> Axon.dense(16, activation: :relu)
    |> Axon.dense(1)

  batches = Enum.zip(Nx.to_batched(xtr, 32), Nx.to_batched(Nx.reshape(Nx.tensor(ytr), {:auto, 1}), 32))
  optimizer = Polaris.Optimizers.adamw(learning_rate: 0.005, decay: 0.01)

  trained =
    model
    |> Axon.Loop.trainer(:mean_squared_error, optimizer, log: 0, seed: 1)
    |> Axon.Loop.run(batches, Axon.ModelState.empty(), epochs: 100)

  params = case trained do
    %{step_state: %{model_state: state}} -> state
    state -> state
  end

  predict = &(model |> Axon.predict(params, &1) |> Nx.to_flat_list())
  {predict.(xte), predict.(xtr)}
end

pick = fn list, ids -> Enum.map(ids, &Enum.at(list, &1)) end

# Ordinary kriging of the training residuals, predicted at the test locations (PyKrige's automatic
# exponential variogram, fitted on the training residuals of each fold).
krige_residuals = fn train_xy, residuals, test_xy ->
  k = Kriging.ordinary(train_xy, residuals, variogram_model: :exponential, nlags: 8)
  {xs, ys} = Enum.unzip(test_xy)
  {z, _} = Kriging.execute(k, :points, xs, ys)
  z
end

cross_validate = fn folds, model, rows, krige? ->
  all = Enum.to_list(0..(n - 1))

  predictions =
    for fold <- 0..4, reduce: %{} do
      acc ->
        train = Enum.filter(all, &(Enum.at(folds, &1) != fold))
        test = Enum.filter(all, &(Enum.at(folds, &1) == fold))
        {xtr, xte} = standardise.(pick.(rows, train), pick.(rows, test))
        ytr = pick.(y, train)
        {p, fitted} = model.(xtr, ytr, xte)

        p =
          if krige? do
            residuals = Enum.zip_with(ytr, fitted, &(&1 - &2))
            Enum.zip_with(p, krige_residuals.(pick.(xy, train), residuals, pick.(xy, test)), &(&1 + &2))
          else
            p
          end

        test |> Enum.zip(p) |> Enum.into(acc)
    end

  errors = Enum.map(0..(n - 1), fn i -> predictions[i] - Enum.at(y, i) end)
  :math.sqrt(Enum.sum(Enum.map(errors, &(&1 * &1))) / n)
end

:ok
```

The cross-validation of every model, with both kinds of folds (the slow cell: the neural networks
train ten times each):

```elixir
models = [
  {"mean", mean_model, x, false},
  {"ridge: features", ridge, x, false},
  {"ridge: features + coordinates", ridge, x_and_coords, false},
  {"KNN: location", knn, coords, false},
  {"MLP: features", mlp, x, false},
  {"MLP: features + coordinates", mlp, x_and_coords, false}
]

results =
  for {name, model, rows, krige?} <- models do
    %{
      model: name,
      random_rmse: cross_validate.(random_fold, model, rows, krige?),
      spatial_rmse: cross_validate.(spatial_fold, model, rows, krige?)
    }
  end

DF.new(results)
```

```elixir
bars =
  for r <- results, {scheme, v} <- [random: r.random_rmse, spatial: r.spatial_rmse],
      do: %{model: r.model, validation: Atom.to_string(scheme), rmse: v}

VegaLite.new(width: 520, height: 300, title: "Cross-validated RMSE of log(price)")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(bars)
|> VegaLite.mark(:bar)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "model", type: :nominal, sort: nil, axis: [label_angle: -30])
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x_offset, "validation", type: :nominal)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "rmse", type: :quantitative, title: "RMSE")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "validation", type: :nominal)
```

Three lessons in one table (RMSE of the log price):

* **The house features carry the prediction.** Ridge regression on the 13 features (0.37) beats
  every model of location alone, and the plain kriging of the Baltimore case (0.45 in
  leave-one-out): what a house is matters more than where it is.
* **Random folds flatter the models that use location.** KNN on the coordinates scores 0.45 with
  random folds but 0.58 with spatial blocks, barely better than the mean (0.61); the neural network
  with coordinates goes from 0.41 to 0.51. Within an area, a sale's neighbours are in the training
  set and the model only has to look them up; in a new area there is nothing to look up. The
  feature-only models score the same either way (ridge 0.37 and 0.37), which is what an honest
  model should do.
* **The neural network does not beat the linear model here.** With 211 sales and 13 features, the
  MLP (0.41 with both kinds of folds) overfits where the regularised linear model does not; deep
  learning needs far more data than this.

## 4. Is there anything left for kriging?

The variogram of the residuals of the ridge model on all sales, next to the variogram of the log
prices themselves:

```elixir
{x_std, _} = standardise.(x, x)
{_, fitted_all} = ridge.(x_std, y, x_std)
residuals = Enum.zip_with(y, fitted_all, &(&1 - &2))

v_price = Variogram.new(xy, y, n_lags: 12, maxlag: 0.5, model: :exponential, use_nugget: true)
v_resid = Variogram.new(xy, residuals, n_lags: 12, maxlag: 0.5, model: :exponential, use_nugget: true)

curves =
  for {name, v} <- [{"log price", v_price}, {"ridge residuals", v_resid}],
      {b, e} <- Enum.zip(v.bins, v.experimental), e != :nan,
      do: %{series: name, lag: b, gamma: e}

VegaLite.new(width: 520, height: 280, title: "Variograms: prices and model residuals")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(curves)
|> VegaLite.mark(:line, point: true)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "lag", type: :quantitative, title: "distance (map units)")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "gamma", type: :quantitative, title: "semivariance")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "series", type: :nominal)
```

```elixir
%{
  pykrige_variogram_of_residuals: Kriging.ordinary(xy, residuals, variogram_model: :exponential, nlags: 8).variogram_model_parameters
}
```

The residual variogram is lower and much flatter than that of the prices: the features have taken
up the spatial pattern. PyKrige's automatic fit makes it pure nugget (no partial sill), so kriging
the residuals can only add their mean.

Regression kriging: the ridge model and the neural network on the features, plus their kriged
residuals:

```elixir
kriged =
  for {name, model} <- [{"ridge: features + kriged residuals", ridge}, {"MLP: features + kriged residuals", mlp}] do
    %{model: name, random_rmse: cross_validate.(random_fold, model, x, true), spatial_rmse: cross_validate.(spatial_fold, model, x, true)}
  end

DF.new(results ++ kriged)
```

The kriged residuals leave both models as they were (ridge 0.368 and 0.367, MLP 0.41): there is no
spatial structure left to use once the features are in.

## What we learned

* With spatial data, **validate by spatial blocks**: random folds understated the error of a
  location-only model by almost a quarter (0.45 against 0.58).
* Model the **features** first; here a regularised linear model does it best, and a neural network
  needs more data than 211 sales.
* Check the **residual variogram**: if it is flat, as here, kriging has nothing to add; if not,
  regression kriging (model + kriged residuals) recovers the spatial part the model missed.
* Machine learning and geostatistics work together: the variogram sized the validation blocks,
  diagnosed the residuals and supplied the kriging step.

Every Geostatex function used here is verified against scikit-gstat or PyKrige (see the parity
guide); Scholar and Axon are the Elixir ML libraries.
